  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nDespite some of the increased troughiness out west, the mid level\nridge holds its ground and corrals the instability over the \nhigher terrain Monday. The ridge does weaken slightly but it \nposition directly overhead helps keep temperatures persistent with\nthose on Sunday. A weak front crosses the Red River Monday and \nstalls over West Central TX by Tuesday. This persistent pattern of\nweakness to the north continues to bring more instability over \nthe higher terrain counties with some of the winds aloft pushing \nmore moisture south Tuesday. This gives most of the forecast area \na chance for some convection, but only isolated coverage \nunderneath the ridge and without a distinct surface focus. While \nit will be nice to see some more areas getting rain, the down side\nis we'll continue to experience heat index values exceeding 100 \nat times.\n\nThe upper ridge appears to be a bit stronger over the area later \nin the week, but there is still a good moisture pooling and \nonshore flow to promote isolated convection. Outside the 7 day \nforecast, deterministic models continue to suggest an upper ridge \nbreakdown in the 8-10 day time-frame, but only to push it later \nwith each run. If this trend continues, we may have to endure the \nentire month of September under the heat wave.",
